  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Simplify the expression $¬(A⇒B)∧¬A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression using the properties of logical operation\n\n- First, we use that $X⇒Y≡¬X∨Y:$\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)∧¬A≡¬(¬A∨B)∧¬A\n$$\n\n- Next, we use a De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)∧¬A≡(¬¬A∧¬B)∧¬A\n$$\n\n- Then, we simplify the double negation:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∧¬B)∧¬A≡(A∧¬B)∧¬A\n$$\n\n- Next, we use  the commutative law:\n\n$$\n(A∧¬B)∧¬A≡(¬B∧A)∧¬A\n$$\n\n- Then, we use the associative law:\n\n$$\n(¬B∧A)∧¬A≡¬B∧(A∧¬A)\n$$\n\n- Next, we use the fact that the conjunction of a statement and its negation is a contradiction:\n\n$$\n¬B∧(A∧¬A)≡¬B∧c\n$$\n\n- Finally, we use the fact that the conjunction of a statement and a contradiction gives a contradiction:\n\n$$\n¬B∧c≡c\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)∧¬A≡c.\n$$",
